+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSocket delivery for committed board changes
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed edit one event revision, and browsers display a revisioned view. Committed revisions must reach connected browsers within the project's 15-second freshness goal, preferably faster, across 800 simultaneous browsers and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second, and a reconnecting browser must recover the latest board after up to 20 minutes offline.
+
+The command path does not fix how committed revisions reach browsers. Delivery can pull from the browser on a fixed interval, push from a socket server the team runs, or push through a hosted realtime provider. The choice decides what the change store exposes to readers, what the delivery node operates, and what browser sync implements, so it is settled before those three nodes are designed.
+
+If the choice is wrong, the cost is spread across all three downstream nodes: a pull mechanism shapes the store around cursor queries and the browser around a loop; a hosted push binds the store, delivery, and browser sync to a vendor's subscription and reconciliation model; a self-managed push makes the team own connection capacity and reconnect behavior.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No candidate fails a hard constraint — all three can meet the freshness goal, the browser and burst loads, and the reconnect-recovery need — so no gate eliminates an option. The choice is made on the weighted criteria below.
+
+- **Update latency** — *moderate*. How quickly a committed revision becomes visible to connected browsers. All candidates clear the 15-second goal, so this weighs the margin beyond it: a smaller margin reduces the window in which a browser shows a stale board.
+- **Implementation simplicity** — *minor*. How much new machinery the change store, delivery, and browser sync must each carry.
+- **Low recurring operating cost** — *moderate*. Whether steady-state delivery incurs a vendor bill on top of the infrastructure already run.
+- **Provider independence** — *major*. Whether the realtime path avoids dependence on a vendor's subscription model, credentials, and publication reconciliation.
+- **Low team operational burden** — *major*. How much connection capacity, session lifecycle, publication reliability, and reconnect/catch-up the team must build and run itself. This criterion cuts against a self-managed push and is named here because the choice turns on how much of it the project is willing to own.
+- **Delivery reliability and failure visibility** — *moderate*. Whether revisions keep flowing and a connectivity failure surfaces rather than silently showing an unchanged board. Its importance derives from the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers request the board, or the changes since the last revision they have seen, over HTTPS every five seconds. Delivery is request/response and the server is stateless between requests. The change store exposes a cursor query so a browser can ask for revisions after a known point; a reconnecting browser recovers simply by requesting from an old cursor.
+
+- **Update latency**: *weak* — up to five seconds of lag, and a burst of edits may arrive coalesced. It clears the 15-second goal without margin.
+- **Implementation simplicity**: *strong* — no persistent connections or session lifecycle; the browser runs a loop and the store answers cursor queries.
+- **Low recurring operating cost**: *strong* — rides the existing HTTPS infrastructure; no vendor bill.
+- **Provider independence**: *strong* — no external dependency.
+- **Low team operational burden**: *strong* — nothing persistent to operate.
+- **Delivery reliability and failure visibility**: *partial* — a failed request retries, but a stalled loop shows a stale board unless freshness is surfaced; the revision cursor makes recovery an ordinary request.
+
+Simplest and cheapest to run, but the few-second lag and repeated reads work against the fast-update goal.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server holds an open WebSocket connection per connected browser and pushes each committed revision as it lands. Delivery manages the sessions; the change store exposes replayable revisions and a reliable publication path so a committed revision is not lost between commit and push; a reconnecting browser resumes from its last seen revision and the server replays what it missed.
+
+- **Update latency**: *strong* — each committed revision is pushed on commit, sub-second.
+- **Implementation simplicity**: *partial* — needs a session layer, a replayable revision feed, and reconnect/catch-up logic across delivery and browser sync.
+- **Low recurring operating cost**: *strong* — no vendor bill; runs on infrastructure already operated.
+- **Provider independence**: *strong* — the realtime path is the team's own.
+- **Low team operational burden**: *weak* — the team owns connection capacity for 800 browsers and opening bursts, session lifecycle, publication reliability, and reconnect/catch-up. This is the cost the choice accepts.
+- **Delivery reliability and failure visibility**: *partial* — publication and reconnect must be built so no committed revision is lost and a dropped connection is surfaced; replayable revisions make catch-up possible.
+
+Fast and vendor-free, at the cost of owning connection and reconnect operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A vendor runs the push infrastructure. Delivery wraps the provider and reconciles the store's publications with it; browser sync uses the provider's subscription model; the provider owns connection capacity and much of the reconnect mechanics.
+
+- **Update latency**: *strong* — the provider pushes updates with low latency.
+- **Implementation simplicity**: *strong* — the provider supplies the transport and subscription model, so less connection machinery is written.
+- **Low recurring operating cost**: *weak* — recurring provider fees.
+- **Provider independence**: *weak* — the realtime path depends on the provider's subscription model, credentials, and a publication reconciliation step.
+- **Low team operational burden**: *strong* — connection capacity and much of reconnect are handled by the provider.
+- **Delivery reliability and failure visibility**: *partial* — the provider handles transport failures, but publication must be reconciled with it and a publication failure must still surface.
+
+Quickest to low latency, but couples the board to a vendor and adds recurring fees.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets** is chosen. It is the only option that is strong on the two major criteria — provider independence and the low-latency behavior the fast-update goal drives — while accepting the one major cost the project is willing to carry: owning connection and reconnect operations.
+
+No option dominates. Polling is strong on provider independence, cost, and simplicity but weak on latency; its few-second lag and repeated reads are what make delivery worth revisiting at all. A hosted service is strong on latency, simplicity, and operational burden but weak on provider independence and recurring cost, which the project declines. The choice is therefore the trade between the weighted major criteria, and the project resolves it in favor of owning operations rather than coupling delivery to a vendor.
+
+Committing to this:
+- **Forecloses** request/response-only delivery. The change store must expose replayable revisions and a reliable publication path, delivery must manage socket sessions, and browser sync must reconnect and catch up. Moving to polling or a vendor later would rework all three nodes.
+- **Obligates** connection capacity for 800 concurrent browsers and opening bursts, publication reliability so a committed revision survives commit-to-push, reconnect/catch-up that recovers up to 20 minutes of missed revisions, and visible connectivity failure rather than a silently stale board.
+- **Turns on** the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*, which the publication-failure and reconnect obligations serve. It operates within *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted*, which keeps both self-hosted and managed families admissible but does not itself prefer self-hosting; the preference for owning the realtime path comes from the weighted criteria, not from a stated tenet.
